sigma_total": 6.83, "baseline_margin": -6.05, "analyst_adjustment": 0.55, "key_factors": ["R+6 district lean and incumbent Flood's fundraising edge ($2.4M vs $0.6M)", "Most raters call it Likely or Solid R, though a few say Lean R", "September Democratic-sponsored poll shows a 43-42 race, far tighter than May polling", "Polymarket prices Flood around 82%"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Flood win: a Republican-leaning district, a well-funded incumbent, and a rating consensus around Likely R. A recent Democratic-sponsored live-caller poll showing a one-point race and an active debate cycle justify shading toward the competitive end, but markets and raters still favor Flood clearly.", "watch": "Whether independent or late-September polling confirms the tight September survey, or whether the race reverts to the district's Republican lean.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Republican Mike Flood seeks re-election in a district that leaned Republican by about six points at the last presidential election, against Democrat Chris Backemeyer. Flood holds a large financial advantage, having raised roughly four times as much as Backemeyer, and most expert raters place the race in the Likely or Solid Republican column, though a couple call it only Lean Republican. Polymarket prices Flood's chances around 82 percent.\n\nThe race has drawn attention: the candidates met in a widely covered late-September debate, clashing over Iran, tariffs, Medicaid and prices. A Democratic-sponsored poll in early September showed a one-point contest, much closer than spring surveys that had Flood up seven to eight. The forecast lands between those signals, expecting a Flood win by mid-single digits with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "